did you ever daydream of being a  superhero as a child as the protector of  justice and defender of all that is good  most kids did i'm sure making your own  secret bases with your best buds and  sharing in adolescent fantasies crafted  around the stories you read in comics  and manga  far from being unusual these are the  common occurrences that make us look  upon our childhoods with a familiar  nostalgia  but of course when making those heroic  tales there's no way you can have a  great hero without a cunning villain to  act as their foil  who is light without l young wenly  without reinhard gone without the  perverted and depraved clown man  and so you would construct these cool  complicated villainous schemes that the  great hero would have to battle against  huge robots anti-proton bombs killer  viruses that sucked the blood out of  your body akin to the horrors of bram  stroker's dracula  and then  you grew up  you witnessed those fragments of your  imagination disappear with the other  childish hopes you once had and you  submit yourself to the all-encompassing  system that crushes hopes and subjects  you to the monotony over the world  life  but what if those nefarious ideas you  thought up for the villain  weren't dismissed as the naive  aspirations of young children what if  you woke up one day only to realize that  a man was beginning to carry out those  villainous schemes you once daydreamed  of to their most horrifyingly real  extent  huge robots  bombs  killer viruses  those childhood fantasies are now coming  to life and terrorizing the world  and you and your friends are the only  ones who might have the key to stopping  it all as the ones who created this plan  so many years ago  this is the story of 20th century boys  by naoki orozawa renowned mangaka and  creator of other beloved manga series  monster  the story follows endo kenji a normal  convenient store manager just trying to  get by  he's long forgotten many of those lofty  aspirations he once had as most kids do  he abandoned his teen love for the  guitar and rock and roll and having to  take care of his missing sister's baby  daughter ties him down to a life he's  not particularly satisfied with  however things quickly begin to take a  turn in this ordinary life when one of  his childhood friends donkey  yes that that was his nickname donkey  commits suicide  and a new cult begins to extend its  reach into every crack and crevice of  society led by a charismatic man they  call  friend  and kenji soon realizes that this cult  is enacting a scheme  far more sinister than it seems on the  surface  world domination  and it's all according to the master  plan that kenji and his friends once  drew up as children  as kenji becomes tangled up in vast  conspiracies and dangerous mysteries he  is left with no other choice but to  abandon the peace he once had in life  in order to wage war against this  overwhelming power that his friend and  his followers and prevent what they will  enact onto the world  kenji must gather his old friends who  have all embarked on their own paths of  life and unexpectedly  become the heroes of justice they once  aspired to be  [Music]  just as naoki orozawa did with monster  before this not only does he write  compelling and tragic main characters  but even more so brilliant and eternally  memorable villains  follows in the footsteps of johann  liebert by being one of the most utterly  mysterious  compelling sadistic and devilish  antagonists to step foot onto a manga  page  in this way both stories follow a  similar narrative in that the hero tenma  and monster and kenji and his friends  and 20th century boys must unravel the  twisted story of their respective  nemeses who were ironically birthed out  of the actions of the heroes themselves  and they must stop them  it sets the foundation for a beautiful  dynamic between the two main characters  in a sense as the protagonist carries  the burden of guilt for every action the  antagonist takes knowing that they were  the ones who allowed this to happen  friend's desire to rule the world is  both insatiable and ingenious with the  funny contrast being that his intricate  master plan is simply taken out of the  old drawings of a group of pre-pubescent  boys in the 70s  in this sense kenji and his friends lay  out the plan for exactly how anyone with  enough determination could rule the  world as friend follows just that but  how are you supposed to rule the world  without knowing all of its languages  this video about 20th century boys is  brought to you by rosetta stone an  excellent language learning program that  can immerse you in no time in whatever  language you choose  from  [Music]  [Music]  if you want to watch anime without  subtitles or simply just learn japanese  or any other language for that matter  or susumedial  the lifetime subscription for rosetta  stone is normally 299 dollars but if you  click on the link in my description you  get it almost 50  off this means that you pay only 180 for  interactive japanese study materials for  life you can take it at whatever pace  you want and only have to pay once for a  lifetime of learning and if you end up  buying it and realize that you don't  want it it's all okay because there's  still a 30 day refund for your full  money back so click on my exclusive link  below if you're interested otherwise  friend will have you purged  i'm joking  or am i  one of the most beautiful aspects of  20th century boys and naoki odasawa  stories in general is exactly that  the unpredictability of characters and  the blurred lines that they have between  good and evil  though it often feels in stories that  anyone against the protagonist is evil  for example many shonen manga make this  distinction simple and obvious for the  sake of building up a brave and strong  hero who stood up against the  incarnation of evil itself  one of the main lines of questioning  that 20th century boys investigates  is who is good kenji and his friends are  fighting to protect the world yes  but they're defending it from their own  machinations  on the other hand friends plan though it  does include taking innumerable lives  and hurting millions  is one that also unites the lost souls  of the world under his cult's wing under  the guise that he is in fact their  friend  and who's to say that he actually isn't  further adding to this 20th century boys  follows the cast of characters through  many different areas of their lives with  time skips aplenty going from 1969 as  children all the way until 2018  this allows us to personally witness the  changes in each character over time and  how their upbringing has molded them  into who they are in the future how the  battle against friend changes them their  hopes and their idea of what is truly  evil  as exemplified in the manga's title much  of the story revolves around kenji and  his friends when they were young boys as  the root of  well  everything  like how the rise of rock music in the  late 60s made kenji into a hopeful  musician and firm believer and the  culture of freedom and authenticity that  the music brought with it  likewise after learning bits and pieces  of friend's story you begin to see his  motives what it stems from and why he  would use kenji's childhood plan of all  things to enact world domination  all of kenji's friends have traveled  their own paths as adults  not all of them are fully on board to  risk their lives to supposedly save the  world they have families or they've left  the past behind them  this story is one of the few times where  the entirety of its huge cast truly have  their own personality and my opinion  their own arcs and stories that truly  make the title 20th century boys  befitting rather than focusing on a  single one of the boys or a single  character  in fact i'd say that a lot of the manga  doesn't even directly follow kenji  though he and friend are at the  epicenter of it all urasawa's attention  to everyone else fleshes out this story  in a non-linear and immensely  imaginative way  and to return to the idea of blurred  lines between good and evil when you  look at all these other members of the  cast the story asks  are you good if you decide not to  abandon everything to take up arms  against a supervillain in what seems  like little more than a childhood  fantasy and instead stay back to support  your family  or are you evil for choosing not to save  the world when you had the chance  is kenji evil for bringing his friends  into this risking their lives  when the burden of it all really weighs  on his shoulders  these are the questions you consider as  you read the story and it's the  questions the characters themselves  consider as well  many of the characters have a sense of  guilt as they debate these questions but  they never really get answered  because of course  it's simply not that easy  because of this tender balance between  good and evil kenji and friend are not  like light and darkness  not opposites but  more akin to two sides of the same coin  more so than anything else  20th century boys reflects the  importance of life in many different  facets of that meaning  for one simply the importance of  persistence in life  what's great about witnessing a story  that occurs over such a long period in  these characters lives is that you can  see them fall and witness whether they  stay down or get back up and keep trying  against an all-encompassing almost  omniscient force like friend with allies  in every corner how can you keep telling  yourself that you can win  how can you devote your life to a battle  you may never see the end of  but a great manga never shoves these  ideas down your throat  20th century boys does it through subtle  details  they ask these questions through the  discussion of a game of bowling  or over a measly bowl of ramen only a  few words shared  but you begin to understand the  importance of self-reflection once you  get older and as these characters get  older as well  considering what has brought you to  where you are and whether this is what  makes you happy  and is there a point to doing it if it  doesn't  how lasting relationships make life  worth living and the importance of  forgetting and forgiving others and  atoning over time  there is so much to take away from this  story and even its smallest moments  it makes you reconsider the way you've  lived your life  and the impact that your formative years  have on who you become  more than telling you the explicit way  to rule the world  20th century boys offers insight into  how perhaps the key to making the world  you want to live in and molding it to  your own ideas  lies in your imagination  it's a reed that can't be passed on  if you've got this far thank you for  being patient while i kind of took a  break from the channel i've got more in  store over the summer and  keep an eye out it'll be great  also do check out rosetta stone if  you're interested make sure to click on  the link below in my bio thanks guys  as always this has been the anime  culture corner i hope you enjoyed the  video don't forget to like and subscribe  for future in-depth show manga and  character analyses
 

